================================================================================
NumPy (the .npy format; no code copied)
================================================================================

NPYFile reads and writes the .npy container as documented in NumPy's
numpy/lib/format.py (NEP 1). NumPy is BSD-3-Clause.


================================================================================
Where the parts come from
================================================================================

- The cube model and the reducers (data source, resident cube, virtual detector,
  the VirtualMask and DPStatistics Metal kernels) were extracted from the GPL-3.0
  macOS app mac4DSTEM (github.com/mac4DSTEM/mac4DSTEM, commit 7448401b) and
  rewritten as a public API without the app's types. mac4DSTEM is the first app
  built on the kit.
- The detector definitions are a port of py4DSTEM's make_detector, and the
  processing follows its get_virtual_image, get_dp_mean, get_dp_max,
  get_probe_size, get_origin and DPC code (GPL-3.0, commit f050d207, 0.14.19).
  py4DSTEM is also the reference the tests compare with.
- The .npy reader and writer follow NumPy's documented format (BSD-3-Clause); no
  NumPy code is copied.

Parallax alignment slice
------------------------
CuboParallax's matrix-DFT refinement and Fourier alignment are adapted from
mac4DSTEM c809679c8ee23a355da6af4f63665aaf64f90d0d,
mac4DSTEM/Core/Compute/MatrixDFTCorrelation.swift and
mac4DSTEM/Core/Analysis/ParallaxAlignment.swift (GPL-3.0), and follow
py4DSTEM f050d207851f8accd3aa550365baaf1f38230428, process/utils/multicorr.py,
process/utils/cross_correlate.py, preprocess/utils.py and process/phase/parallax.py.
Matrix-DFT method: Guizar-Sicairos, Thurman and Fienup, Optics Letters 33,
156-158 (2008). Public test-data crop: py4DSTEM small_datacube.dm4; source
SHA-256 and selection are recorded in the fixture.

Fourier iDPC additions:
IDPC's numerical integration follows mac4DSTEM c809679c, file
Core/Analysis/DPC.swift (GPL-3.0). Its regression oracle is an explicitly named
NumPy float64 transcription, distinct from py4DSTEM iterative DPC.
